ol systems. Particular attention is given to estimating and using the State of Health (SoH). It also highlights a need for a Battery Safety Management System (BSMS) in addition to BMS for energy system storage, sustainability, and safety.\n\n  * Abstract\n  * Full text\n  * PDF\n  * References\n\nOpen Access\n\n[Input]\n\nA Multi-Stage Optimal Allocation of Newly Added Electric Vehicle Charging Station Based on Comprehensive Service Performance\n\nYufei Wang,  Zheng Liu,  Donglin Liu,  Hua Xue,  Yiyan Sang,  Shunfu Lin, \n\n  * First Published: 06 October 2025\n\nThis study proposes a multi-stage optimal allocation method for newly added electric vehicle (EV) charging stations, integrating probabilistic user behaviour analysis, fuzzy-based service performance evaluation, and economic optimisation. Simulation results demonstrate that the proposed approach effectively balances service performance and investment cost, supporting rational infrastructure planning with improved overall system performance.\n\n  * Abstract\n  * Full text\n  * PDF\n  * References\n  * Request permissions\n\nOpen Access\n\n[Input]\n\nTransient Stability Improvement of Power System by Assigning Dynamic Converter Limits to Superconducting Magnetic Energy Storage\n\nAbdul Waheed Kumar,  Mairaj Ud-Din Mufti,  Mohd Hasan Ali, \n\n  * First Published: 18 November 2025\n\n  * Abstract\n  * Full text\n  * PDF\n  * References\n  * Request permissions\n\n[Input]\n\nNumerical Investigation of a Hybrid Phase Change Material–Heat Pipe Battery Thermal Management System for Enhanced Thermal Regulation under High Discharge Conditions\n\nSowdagar Mohammed Shehabaz,  Santhosh Kumar Gugulothu,  Raju Muthyala,  Pemmanaboyina Vishnu,  Peketi Buliraju, \n\n  * First Published: 10 August 2025\n\nThis study presents a validated numerical investigation of a hybrid battery thermal management system integrating phase change material and heat pipes.   * Abstract\n  * Full text\n  * PDF\n  * References\n  * Request permissions\n\n[Input]\n\nAdvances in Molybdenum Disulfide (MoS_{2}) and its Composites: Pioneering Structures, Synthesis, and Quantum Capacitive Performance for Next-Gen Supercapacitors\n\nMuzahir Iqbal,  Abhijeet Singh,  Nilesh G. Saykar,  Anil Arya,  Chandrashekhar M. Mahajan,  S. K. Mahapatra, \n\n  * First Published: 31 July 2025\n\nThis review highlights recent advances in MoS_{2}-based supercapacitors, integrating both theoretical modelling and experimental performance MoS_{2} and its composite-based material. It discusses structure–property relationships, charge storage mechanisms, and strategies for enhancing capacitance. The synergy between DFT calculations and material engineering offers a roadmap for next-generation energy storage devices.\n\n  * Abstract\n  * Full text\n  * PDF\n  * References\n  * Request permissions\n\n[Input]\n\nSulfur Vacancy-Engineered NiCoB/ZnIn_{2}S_{4} Schottky Junctions for Synergistic Charge Separation and Photocatalytic Hydrogen Evolution\n\nKai Wang,  Tiantian Bai,  Qing Chen,  Qin Luo,  Yaoyao Chen,  Zhiliang Jin, \n\n  * First Published: 29 August 2025\n\nIn this study, a novel composite catalyst NiCoB/ZnIn_{2}S_{4}-Vs, composed of sulfur vacancy-containing ZnIn_{2}S_{4}-Vs and NiCoB particles, was designed. The composite material exhibited a 3D flower-like nanostructure, which boosted the density of active sites; 2D nanosheet self-assembly-induced light reflection/scattering enhanced the composite's light-harvesting capability; NiCoB/ZnIn_{2}S_{4}-Vs interface Schottky heterojunction promoted charge carrier separation.\n\n  * Abstract\n  * Full text\n  * PDF\n  * References\n  * Request permissions\n\nOpen Access\n\n[Input]\n\nMulti-Objective Optimization of a Climate-Responsive Green Hydrogen-Based Multi-Generation System with Advanced Energy Storage and Heat Recovery\n\nEhsanolah Assareh,  Mohtada Sadrzadeh,  Nima Izadyar,  Elmira Jamei,  Saleh Mobayen,  Mehrdad Ahmadinejad, \n\n  * First Published: 10 September 2025\n\nA novel hydrogen-based multi-generation system integrates CAES, PEME, and ORC for climate-responsive power generation. Using response surface methodology, the system is optimized for energy efficiency, emissions, and cost. Results highlight its strong performance in temperate climates and its potential for modular, scalable deployment.\n\n  * Abstract\n  * Full text\n  * PDF\n  * References\n  * Request permissions\n\n[Input]\n\nResearch Progress and Application Prospects of Transition Metal Phosphosulfide Heterojunction Catalysts in Electrocatalytic Water Splitting\n\nNingning Liu,  Changyi Deng,  Tingting Huang,  Hui Ding,  Prof. Li Zhang,  Prof. Guancheng Xu, \n\n  * First Published: 24 August 2025\n\nA schematic overview of the core themes of this review, covering the advantages of heterojunctions, synthesis methods, and optimization strategies for transition metal phosphosulfide heterojunctions. It separately analyzes the catalytic mechanisms of the hydrogen evolution reaction (HER) and the oxygen evolution reaction (OER), and explores the optimization of the adsorption–desorption of HER and OER intermediates by transition metal phosphosulfide heterostructures.\n\n  * Abstract\n  * Full text\n  * ePub\n  * PDF\n  * References\n  * Request permissions\n\n[Input]\n\nSynthesis, Characterisation, and Catalytic Evaluation of Novel Mesoporous Zeolite H-BEA Catalysts for n-Hexyl Levulinate Synthesis: Effect of Templates and Kinetic Studies\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPowering the Future: AI, Innovation, and America’s Clean Energy Transformation – Nova Science Publishers (https://novapublishers.com/shop/powering-the-future-ai-innovation-and-americas-clean-energy-transformation/)\nciteturn328search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 weeks ago; \n\n# Powering the Future: AI, Innovation, and America’s Clean Energy Transformation\n\n$170.00\n\nLeanna Moon (Editor)\n\nSeries: Energy Policies, Politics and Prices; Renewable Energy: Research, Development and Policies\nBISAC: POL068000; TEC031010; SCI024000\n\nAs the U.S. races toward a 100% clean electricity future, artificial intelligence and digital innovation are redefining how energy is produced, delivered, and consumed. Powering the Future brings together key analyses from the U.S. Department of Energy, exploring how AI, modern grid infrastructure, and forward-looking policy are shaping a sustainable energy economy.\n\nFrom transmission modernization and interagency reliability efforts to the challenge of powering AI and data centers responsibly, this book captures the dynamic interplay between technology, policy, and workforce transformation and offers a clear, data-driven view of the opportunities and challenges facing America’s clean energy transition.\n\nBinding  | [Select]Clear\n\nPowering the Future: AI, Innovation, and America’s Clean Energy Transformation quantity [Input: ]\n\n[Button: Add to cart]\n\nsomdn_product_page\n\nISBN: N/A Categories: 2026, Books, Energy Policies, Politics and Prices, Environment & Energy, Newly Published Books, Renewable Energy, Science and Technology, SNOVA, Technology and Engineering\n\n## Table of Contents\n\nPreface\n\nChapter 1. On The Path to 100% Clean Electricity\nU.S. Department of Energy\n\nChapter 2. Investing in American Energy: Continued Progress Through Policy\nU.S. Department of Energy\n\nChapter 3. Transmission Impact Assessment: Power Sector Infrastructure Deployment to Reduce Costs, Improve Reliability, and Lower Pollution\nU.S. Department of Energy\n\nChapter 4. Joint Memorandum on Interagency Communication and Consultation on Electric Reliability\nU.S. Department of Energy and U.S. Environmental Protection Agency\n\nChapter 5. AI for Energy: Opportunities for a Modern Grid and Clean Energy Economy\nU.S. Department of Energy\n\nChapter 6. Recommendations on Powering Artificial Intelligence and Data Center Infrastructure\nU.S. Department of Energy\n\nChapter 7. 2025 United States E