d might provide in the future. Table SI.1 and Figure SI.1 provide a quick summary, which you may want to refer back to. I also found it hard to conceive of energy amounts this large, so I tried to express them as something I could imagine. That’s why Table SI.1 shows how many Boeing 747 round-the-world trips each amount ...\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPowering the Future: A Lawyer's Guide to Clean Energy (https://www.americanbar.org/products/inv/book/444340055/)\nciteturn328search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.2 years ago; Crawled: last month; \n\n# Powering the Future: A Lawyer's Guide to Clean Energy\n\n## By Steven Ferrey\n\n# Powering the Future: A Lawyer's Guide to Clean Energy\n\n## By Steven Ferrey\n\nThis book covers the once-in-a-century shift in key energy technology as the Biden Administration has enacted new laws that shift the entire U.S. economy by 2035 off fossil fuels to electric power. It is a forward-looking guide to how lawyers can successfully utilize key legal opportunities in this critical moment.\n\n# Powering the Future: A Lawyer's Guide to Clean Energy\n\n## By Steven Ferrey\n\n# Powering the Future: A Lawyer's Guide to Clean Energy\n\n## By Steven Ferrey\n\nThis book covers the once-in-a-century shift in key energy technology as the Biden Administration has enacted new laws that shift the entire U.S. economy by 2035 off fossil fuels to electric power. It is a forward-looking guide to how lawyers can successfully utilize key legal opportunities in this critical moment.\n\n$129\n\nNON-MEMBERS\n\n$116\n\nMEMBERS\n\n$103\n\nSECTION MEMBERS\n\n$00\n\nYOUR PRICE\n\n$129\n-Steve Weissman, Lecturer at the UC Berkeley Goldman School of Public Policy, Founder and former Director of the Energy Program at Berkeley Law\n\n\"Steve Ferrey provides a concise 'roadmap' scrutinizing what remains, what is new, what is court-vetted or still to be tested, and what now is legally superseded or preempted for energy, land-use, climate, and environmental law. In sixteen stand-alone chapters, this book analyzes how new laws integrate with existing laws, what states have what particular incentives and clean resources, and how federal, state, and local laws conflict. For any state or federal law that suffers from a gap or deficiency, he provides strategic 'workarounds' to help attorneys and energy professionals transcend each barrier confronted. This book will remain a legal and policy users' manual regardless of political changes.\"\n-Mindy Lubber, CEO & President of CERES, Former Regional Administrator, U.S. EPA\n\nTable of Contents:\n\nPart A: Unique, Inventive, Irreplaceable; Changing the World\n\n  1. Electricity: Why it Now Becomes the Most Important Invention in History\n  2. How Climate Change Simultaneously Changes the Electric Power, Environment and Law\n\nPart B: Strategically Navigating Legal Issues for Power\n\n  3. \"Location, Location, Location\": Where the New Power Resources will be Relocating and Land-Use Legal Challenges\n  4. State & Federal Legal 'Levers' to Regulate Electricity, Climate, Environment – The 'Major Questions' Doctrine\n  5. Supreme Court's Legal Foundation for New Electric Power\n  6. \"Taxman:\" Legal Strategies using New Tax Laws\n  7. Supreme Court's New Mandate: Environmental and Energy Costs\n  8. Renewable Portfolio Standards: Leveraging Law & Subsidies Provided in 60% of the States\n  9. Net Metering: Leveraging Law and Subsidies Provided in 80% of the States\n  10. Cities & States Legally 'Zone-Out' Electric Infrastructure and Environment – Alternative Legal 'Work-Arounds'\n  11. The 'Critical Path' State Legal Barriers Blocking New Renewable Power Infrastructure and Climate Restoration\n\nPart C: Legal Issues Requiring a New Energy Business Model\n\n  12. Moore's Law: Electric Technology and Changing Law\n  13. Legal/Regulatory Changes Mandate a New Business Model\n\nPart D: Below the Radar Screen – What is Coming that One Doesn